File Index

/CapturedPatterns_Reference.txt
Purpose: READ THE "HOW TO SEARCH THIS CORPUS" BLOCK AT THE TOP BEFORE DOING ANY CORPUS WORK AGAINST THIS FILE — three traps produce false negatives and have already caused a retracted pass: the quotes around entries are the DOCUMENT'S not the game's, ANSI colour sits INSIDE phrases, and half the log corpus stores text as escaped JSON (plus a ¤M= framing in raw.log). Searching the quoted forms wrongly marked four entries absent and left two of the doc's own "zero corpus occurrences" notes standing; the Unholy proc alone has 96,825 hits. Master catalog of DSL game-text line patterns mined from DSL2's own .lua, its native XML trigger groups, and PNP-family sibling profiles — combat/condition/death/flee, active and passive skills, and a ~90-spell affect table. Each entry is tagged with its provenance ([ACTIVE — DSL2], [NATIVE ONLY], [UNCONFIRMED]); the spell affect table is victim-side ("You are blinded!") and so cannot detect effects landing on someone else.

/PatternMap.md
Purpose: Superseded first draft of the line→utterance map as markdown tables; GFM requires `|` escaped as `\|` inside a cell even within backticks, so every regex alternation split its row (truncated "Danger Sense" to "Danger ", ate the condition-tier utterance column entirely). Kept for history — PatternMap.yaml is the live document.

/PatternMap.yaml
Purpose: Game line → spoken utterance map for text-to-speech, grouped utterance-first (many patterns, one thing said) so per-utterance facts have a home — notably which capture holds the subject, which a two-column table cannot express (the lifebane line reads "<weapon> coats <victim>", subject group 2). YAML block scalars keep patterns escape-free. Each entry carries speak (announce/raw/suppress), repeat (once = landing, tick = re-fires every round and must be debounced), and status (verified = in the DSL log corpus / unverified = real game text per the reference but not corpus-seen / absent = searched across all 2,478 logs incl. a broadened keyword probe and neither the line nor any related wording occurs, which for a spell usually means the logged characters never had it rather than that the wording is wrong / speculative = extrapolated). Fully corpus-checked as of 2026-08-02 — line entries 49/50 verified, affect table 72 verified + 8 absent, and SIX reference wordings found WRONG rather than merely unlogged (prayer "overtakes the room" not "your presence"; taunt needs DOUBLE spaces before each KILL!!; charm "<X> regains free will" with no possessive; plus third-person forms for warcry, antimagic shell and prevent recovery that the reference never carried). Machine-readable so the plugin matcher tables can eventually be generated from it rather than hand-transcribed across text-to-speech.plugin.ts, combat-compression.plugin.ts, weapon-flag-squelch.plugin.ts and stun-highlight.plugin.ts. TWO SHAPES in one file: `id`-keyed entries for third-person combat text, where the work is extracting WHO (regex + capture groups); and a denser `spell`-keyed self-affect table (~96 spells, onset/wearoff), where who is always you and the work is naming WHAT — there the spell name IS the utterance ("Your eyes tingle." → "Detect magic."), matching is literal substring rather than regex (verified collision-free: one exact duplicate, the protection evil/good/neutral wear-off, and zero substring shadowing), and `kind` sorts buff/debuff/utility/offense. The whole affect table is [PNP SOURCE] with no DSL-corpus backing except detect magic's onset.
